<spell-notebook "transition usage">
	<header level="1">
		Transition
		<header>A transition is an animation used to show or hide content, or to draw attention to it.</>
	</>
	<transition-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#methods-and-commands">Methods and commands</>
			<item link="#queuing-animations">Queuing animations</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#animating-any-element">Animating any element</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Wrap the content to animate in `<ui-transition>`. `visible` drives it: absent means hidden, and each
			change animates `animation` in or out. The first paint never animates.

			- `animation` takes Fomantic's names, spaces and all (`fade up`, `horizontal flip`, `browse right`);
			  an attention one (`shake`, `pulse`...) runs in place and leaves visibility alone.
			- `duration` takes milliseconds (`300`) or a CSS time (`0.3s`); default the animation's own.
			- `inline` makes the box an inline block, for inline content (an image, a button).
			- Transitions are three kinds, as in Fomantic: inward (shows), outward (hides) and static
			  (attention: visibility unchanged).
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Methods and commands">
		<markdown>
			There is no attribute for "shake now", so the host has methods, each resolving once its animation
			has run: `show()`, `hide()`, `toggle()` and `transition(name?)` (Fomantic's
			`$(el).transition(name)`; `animate` is the Web Animations API's). `show()` / `hide()` /
			`toggle()` write `visible`, so it reflects. Invoker commands do the same with no script, from a
			`<ui-button>` or a native `<button>`: `--show`, `--close`, `--toggle` and `--transition`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<ui-button commandfor="notice" command="--toggle">Toggle</ui-button>
			<ui-transition id="notice" animation="fade up" visible>
			  <ui-message>Saved.</ui-message>
			</ui-transition>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Queuing animations">
		<markdown>
			Animations queue, as Fomantic's did: each waits for the one before, and a queued one with no
			direction works it out from the visibility it will have by then. The same animation twice in a
			row is dropped (`allow-repeats` keeps it). With `interrupt`, a new animation stops the running one
			instead (Fomantic's `queue: false`).

			Fomantic's `stop`, `stop all` and `clear queue` behaviours are not ported: `interrupt` covers
			replacing the running animation.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-opened` (`{ visible: true, animation }`): shown, its animation finished (Fomantic's
			  `onVisible`).
			- `ui-closed` (`{ visible: false, animation }`): hidden, its animation finished (`onHidden`).
			- `ui-animated` (`{ visible, animation }`): any animation finished, attention ones included
			  (`onComplete`).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const notice = document.querySelector("#notice")
			await notice.hide() // resolves once animated out
			await notice.transition("shake") // attention:  in place
			notice.addEventListener("ui-opened", (event) => console.log(event.detail.animation))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Animating any element">
		<markdown>
			Other components (dropdowns, modals, popups ...) animate with the same catalogue through the
			runtime's `UI.transitions`, which also plays it on any element without wrapping it. The animations
			themselves are the foundation's `animations.css`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			None of its own: the box takes no focus. Drive transitions from real buttons (`commandfor`), so
			keyboard and screen reader users can reach them.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- Hidden content is out of the page and the accessibility tree, as `hidden` would make it: nothing
			  inside can take focus or be read.
			- Showing or hiding content doesn't announce anything by itself; if the change matters, say so in
			  a live region or move focus to it.
			- Under `prefers-reduced-motion` the element jumps straight to its end state, with the same events.
			- If the element fails to render, it shows its children through a bare `<slot>` and no longer follows
			  `visible`: no showing or hiding, no animation, and no `ui-opened` / `ui-closed` or `ui-animated`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>Bind `visible`, and call the methods through a ref for attention animations.</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			<ui-transition animation="fade up" visible={shown} ref={box} onui-hide={() => setGone(true)}>
			  ...
			</ui-transition>
			// later:  box.current.transition("shake")
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-transition ref="box" animation="fade up" :visible="shown" @ui-closed="gone = true">...</ui-transition>
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<ui-transition
			  animation="fade up"
			  visible={shown()}
			  on-ui-closed={() => setGone(true)}
			>
			  ...
			</ui-transition>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
